{"title":"Lakeside School District","sub":"Tutoring or smaller classes?","type":"Public sector","industry":"Education","geo":"United States","diff":3,"fmt":"Candidate-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Public sector · cost-effectiveness · stakeholder management","prompt":"Lakeside school district has 10,000 primary students; 40% are below the reading standard for their age. A $4.8M state grant must be used to raise reading results this year. Teachers' representatives want smaller classes. The superintendent has seen research on small-group tutoring. You're asked which plan would bring more students up to standard.","clarify":[{"q":"Tutoring?","a":"Three sessions a week in groups of three with trained tutors, for students below standard: about $1,200 per student a year. Local pilots: about 30% of tutored students reached the standard who otherwise wouldn't."},{"q":"Smaller classes?","a":"Cutting class sizes from 28 to 24 costs about $700 per student covered a year (more teachers and rooms). Research suggests about 6% more of the students covered reach the standard."},{"q":"Constraints?","a":"Tutors can be recruited within a term; extra classrooms aren't available in some schools."}],"structure":{"root":"Which plan brings more students up to the reading standard?","branches":[{"head":"Outcome","items":["Students reaching standard who otherwise wouldn't"]},{"head":"Cost per outcome","items":["Tutoring","Smaller classes"]},{"head":"Reach and feasibility","items":["Who each plan covers","Hiring and space"]},{"head":"Stakeholders","items":["Teachers","Parents"]}]},"hypothesis":"Tutoring targets the students who need help, so each dollar should go further. Smaller classes spread money across all students, most of whom are already at standard.","questions":[{"q":"Compare cost per extra student reaching the standard.","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p><b>Tutoring:</b> $1,200 ÷ 30% = <b>$4,000</b> per student who reaches the standard.</p> <p><b>Smaller classes:</b> $700 ÷ 6% = <b>$11,700</b>.</p> <p>Tutoring is about 2.9× as cost-effective.</p>"},{"q":"What would $4.8M achieve under each plan?","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"405.028156pt\" height=\"158.164pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 405.028156 158.164\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g id=\"patch_1\"><path d=\"M 0 158.164 L 405.028156 158.164 L 405.028156 0 L 0 0 L 0 158.164 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"axes_1\"><g id=\"patch_2\"><path d=\"M 51.868156 133.056 L 397.828156 133.056 L 397.828156 11.088 L 51.868156 11.088 L 51.868156 133.056 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"matplotlib.axis_1\"><g id=\"xtick_1\"><g id=\"line2d_1\"><defs><path id=\"mff7d576334\" d=\"M 0 0 L 0 3.5 \" style=\"stroke: #555555; stroke-width: 0.8\"/></defs><g><use xlink:href=\"#mff7d576334\" x=\"137.48452\" y=\"133.056\" style=\"fill: #555555; stroke: #555555; stroke-width: 0.8\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_1\"><g style=\"fill: #555555\" transform=\"translate(119.847332 148.75) scale(0.09 -0.09)\"><defs><path id=\"LibreFranklin-Regular-2f\" d=\"M 269 4678 Q 269 4710 285 4729 Q 301 4749 346 4749 L 3789 4749 Q 3827 4749 3846 4729 Q 3866 4710 3866 4678 L 3866 4346 Q 3866 4282 3789 4282 L 2490 4282 Q 2419 4282 2419 4218 L 2419 83 Q 2419 0 2342 0 L 1882 0 Q 1805 0 1805 83 L 1805 4211 Q 1805 4282 1741 4282 L 346 4282 Q 269 4282 269 4346 L 269 4678 z\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"/><path id=\"LibreFranklin-Regular-6d\" d=\"M 1645 358 Q 1920 358 2173 508 Q 2426 659 2675 915 L 2675 3296 Q 2675 3392 2778 3392 L 3130 3392 Q 3219 3392 3219 3302 L 3206 83 Q 3206 0 3136 0 L 2765 0 Q 2694 0 2694 70 L 2694 448 Q 2694 480 2681 486 Q 2669 493 2637 467 Q 2285 166 2013 51 Q 1741 -64 1472 -64 Q 1178 -64 947 57 Q 717 179 582 400 Q 448 621 448 915 L 448 3290 Q 448 3392 544 3392 L 902 3392 Q 986 3392 986 3302 L 986 1056 Q 986 742 1162 550 Q 1338 3